          %title Quantization
          %h2#Quantization Quantization
          %p In this chapter we discuss the following topics:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#MotivationFromLieTheory"} Motivation from Lie theory
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#GeometricQuantization"} Geometric quantization
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#StarProducts"} Moyal star products
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#MoyalStarProductAsDeformationQuantization"} Moyal star product as deformation quantization
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#MoyalStarProductViaGeometricQuantization"} Moyal star product via geometric quantization
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#WickAlgebraOfNormalOrderedProductsOnKählerVectorspace"} Example: Wick algebra of normal ordered product on Kähler vector space
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#RegularPolynomialFieldTheoryStarProduct"} Star-product on regular polynomial observables in field theory
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            In the previous chapters we had found the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peierls-Poisson+bracket"} Peierls-Poisson bracket
            (theorem
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PPeierlsBracket"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            (prop.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CovariantPhaseSpace"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixed
            (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GaugeFixingLagrangianDensity"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
            (def.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FreeFieldTheory"}
          %p
            This
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SuperPoissonAlgebra"}
            below) is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+bracket"} Lie bracket
            and hence reflects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+symmetries"} infinitesimal symmetries
            acting on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+symmetries+of+the+Lagrangian"} infinitesimal symmetries of the Lagrangian
            and the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST-complex"} BRST
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+phase+space"} reduced
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            (example
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LocalOffShellBRSTComplex"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            symmetries into the very geometry of the phase space by forming their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotient"} homotopy quotient
            given by the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebroid"} Lie algebroid
            (def.
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LInfinityAlgebroid"}
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            to a finite (instead of infinitesimal) structure is called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+flows"} Hamiltonian flows
            hard-wired into its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/master+thesis+Bongers"}> Bongers 14, section 4
            ).
          %p
            Where smooth functions on the plain covariant phase space form the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebra"} commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
            under their pointwise product (def.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Observable"}
            = succeed "-refinement" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+convolution+product"} groupoid convolution product
            and as such become a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebra"} non-commutative
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+quantum+observables"} algebra of quantum observables
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids"} geometric quantization of symplectic groupoids
            (
            %a{:href => "geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids#EH"}> Hawkins 04
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/master+thesis+Nuiten"} Nuiten 13
          %p
            Instead of discussing this in generality, we here focus right away on the simple special case relevant for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theories"} free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theories"} Lagrangian field theories
            in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#FreeQuantumFields"} next chapter
          %p
            After an informal motivation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
            %a{:href => "#MotivationFromLieTheory"} below
            (for a self-contained introduction see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/master+thesis+Bongers"} Bongers 14
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            by discussing how the archetypical example of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+representation"} Schrödinger representation
            arises from the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarized
            action of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            (example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GeometricRepresentationSchroedingerRepresentation"}
            below). With the concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarization
            thus motivated, we use this to find the polarized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+convolution+algebra"} groupoid convolution algebra
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            of a free theory (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PolarizedSymplecticGroupoidConvolutionProductOfSymplecticVectorSpaceIsMoyalStarProduct"}
            below).
          %p
            The result is the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moyal+star+product"}> Moyal
            \-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"}> star product
            ” (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StarPoduct"}
            below). This is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponentiation"} exponentiation
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+kernel"} integral kernel
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            plus possibly a symmetric shift (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SymmetricContribution"}
            below); it turns out to be (example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MoyalStarProductIsFormalDeformationQuantization"}
            below) a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            of the original commutative pointwise product (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FormalDeformationQuantization"}
            below).
          %p
            Below we spell out the (elementary) proofs of these statements for the case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+spaces"} phase spaces
            which are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite dimensional vector spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomials"} polynomials
            and hence go through in more general contexts as long as these basic algebraic properties are retained.
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
            the analogue of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series+algebras"} formal power series algebras
            on a linear
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            is, a priori, the algebra of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+observables"} polynomial observables
            (def.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PolynomialObservables"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomials"} polynomials
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+observables"} field observables
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Φ
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Phi}^a(x)
            (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PointEvaluationObservables"}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions"} distributions
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution+of+several+variables"} of several variables
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+analysis"} microlocal analysis
            %em if
            potential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-divergences"} UV-divergences
            (remark
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UltravioletDivergencesFromPaleyWiener"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+set"} wave front set
            (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#WaveFrontSet"}
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H%C3%B6rmander%27s+criterion"} Hörmander's criterion
            (prop.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HoermanderCriterionForProductOfDistributions"}
          %p
            This criterion is always met on the subspace of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+polynomial+observables"} regular polynomial observables
            and hence every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagator"} propagator
            induces a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
            on these (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PropagatorStarProduct"}
            below). In particular thus the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            of its algebra of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+polynomial+observables"} regular polynomial observables
            (cor.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FreeGaugeFixedLagrangianFieldTheoryQuantizationOfRegularObservables"}
            below). In order to extend this to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observables"} local observables
            one may appeal to a certain quantization freedom (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SymmetricContribution"}
            below) and shift the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            by a symmetric contribution, such that it becomes the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+propagator"} Wightman propagator
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TowardsQuantizationExtendingBeyondRegularPolynomialObservables"}
            at the end below).
          %p
            In conclusion, for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
            the product in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+quantum+observables"} algebra of quantum observables
            is given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential"} exponentiating
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagators"} propagators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorics"} combinatorics
            of these exponentiated propagator expressions that yields the hallmark structures of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick%27s+lemma"} Wick's lemma
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebra"} Wick algebra
            of free fields, and the combinatorics of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            for the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
            %em
              %a{:href => "#FreeQuantumFields"} Free quantum fields
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#Scattering"} Scattering
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal-ordered+product"} normal-ordered product
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebra"} Wick algebra
            (example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#WickAlgebraOfASingleMode"}
            below).
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p#MotivationFromLieTheory
            %strong motivation from Lie theory
          %p
            Quantization of course was and is motivated by experiment, hence by observation of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            correctly account for experimental observations where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            gives no answer or incorrect answers. A historically important example is the phenomenon called the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultraviolet+catastrophe"}> ultraviolet catastrophe
            ”, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paradox"} paradox
            predicted by classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistical+mechanics"} statistical mechanics
            which is
            %em not
            observed in nature, and which is corrected by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %p
            But one may also ask, independently of experimental input, if there are good formal mathematical reasons and motivations to pass from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            by just pondering the mathematical formalism of
            = succeed "?" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
          %p
            The following spells out an argument to this effect. It will work for readers with a background in modern
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
          %p
            So to briefly recall, a system of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum mechanics
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \omega)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+functions"} algebra of functions
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                classical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+bracket"} Lie bracket
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamics"} dynamics
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \in C^\infty(X)
            is the function on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            which is interpreted as assigning to each configuration of the system its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            function – then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            yields the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
            time evolution of the system: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
            famous as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamilton%27s+equations"} Hamilton's equations
          %p
            Something to take notice of here is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimal
            nature of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
            approximation to a globally defined object, the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            (or generally
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+group"} smooth group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            and that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+differentiation"} Lie differentiation
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            Therefore a natural question to ask is:
            %em
              Since the observables in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
              form a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
              under
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
              = succeed "?" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
          %p
            The answer to this is of course “well known” in the literature, in the sense that there are relevant monographs which state the answer. But, maybe surprisingly, the answer to this question is not (at time of this writing) a widely advertized fact that has found its way into the basic educational textbooks. The answer is that this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            which integrates the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            is the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"}> quantomorphism group
            ”, an object that seamlessly leads to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            of the system.
          %p
            Before we spell this out in more detail, we need a brief technical aside: of course
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            is not quite unique. There may be different global
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            objects with the same
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
          %p
            The simplest example of this is already one of central importance for the issue of quantization, namely, the Lie integration of the abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+Lie+algebra"} line Lie algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
            associated with it: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected+topological+space"} simply connected
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/translation+group"} translation group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            itself again, equipped with its canonical additive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            structure, and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            of this by the group of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U(1) = \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Notice that it is the discrete and hence “quantized” nature of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            that makes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            become a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            here. This is not entirely a coincidence of terminology, but can be traced back to the heart of what is “quantized” about
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %p
            Namely, one finds that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔭𝔬𝔦𝔰𝔰
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{poiss}(X,\omega)
            of the classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            is (for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+topological+space"} connected
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+extension"} Lie algebra extension
            of the Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔥𝔞𝔪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{ham}(X)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+fields"} Hamiltonian vector fields
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            by the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+Lie+algebra"} line Lie algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi 𝔭𝔬𝔦𝔰𝔰
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi 𝔥𝔞𝔪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{R} \longrightarrow \mathfrak{poiss}(X,\omega) \longrightarrow \mathfrak{ham}(X)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This means that under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            turns into an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+extension"} central extension
            of the group of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+symplectomorphisms"} Hamiltonian symplectomorphisms
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+extension"} central extension
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            to exist,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            needs to satisfy a quantization condition which says that it admits a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+extension"} central extension
            of the group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ham
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ham(X,\omega)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+symplectomorphisms"} Hamiltonian symplectomorphisms
            exists and is called… the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"} quantomorphism group
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi QuantMorph
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} QuantMorph(X,\omega)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi QuantMorph
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Ham
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U(1) \longrightarrow QuantMorph(X,\omega) \longrightarrow Ham(X,\omega)
                  \\,.
          %p
            While important, for some reason this group is not very well known, which is striking because it contains a small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
            which is famous in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+group"} Heisenberg group
          %p
            More precisely, whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            itself has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+action"} compatible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            structure, notably if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            is just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+space"} symplectic vector space
            (regarded as a group under addition of vectors), then we may ask for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"} quantomorphism group
            which covers the (left)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            on itself. This is the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+group"} Heisenberg group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Heis
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Heis(X,\omega)
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+extension"} central extension
            of the group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            itself:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Heis
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U(1) \longrightarrow Heis(X,\omega) \longrightarrow X
                  \\,.
          %p
            At this point it is worth pausing for a second to note how the hallmark of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            has appeared as if out of nowhere simply by applying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebraic
            structures in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
          %p
            if we think of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integrating
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            to the interesting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            instead of to the uninteresting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/translation+group"} translation group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis"} basis
            element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \in \mathbb{R}
            is canonically “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            ”, the imaginary unit. Therefore one often writes the above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+extension"} central extension
            instead as follows:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi 𝔭𝔬𝔦𝔰𝔰
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi 𝔥𝔞𝔪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  i \mathbb{R} \longrightarrow \mathfrak{poiss}(X,\omega) \longrightarrow \mathfrak{ham}(X,\omega)
          %p
            in order to amplify this. But now consider the simple special case where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega) = (\mathbb{R}^2, d p \wedge d q)
            is the 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+space"} symplectic vector space
            which is for instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            propagating on the line. Then a canonical set of generators for the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            consists of the linear functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            of classical mechanics textbook fame, together with the
            %em constant
            function. Under the above Lie theoretic identification, this constant function is the canonical basis element of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
          %p
            With this notation then the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            manifest, indeed reads
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi q
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [q,p] = i
                  \\,.
          %p
            Since the choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis"} basis
            element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \mathbb{R}
            is arbitrary, we may rescale here the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            by any non-vanishing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            without changing this statement. If we write “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            ” for this element, then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            instead reads
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi q
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi i
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [q,p] = i \hbar
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is of course the hallmark equation for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            here indeed as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
          %p
            This is only the beginning of the story of quantization, naturally understood and indeed “derived” from applying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"} quantomorphism group
            which is the non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            is naturally constructed as follows: given the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            2-form of a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connection"} principal connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+line+bundle"} complex line bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (this is directly analogous to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization"} Dirac charge quantization
            when instead of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            we consider the the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            2-form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            on
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L, \nabla)
            is called a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"} quantomorphism group
            is simply the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+group"} automorphism group
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphisms"} diffeomorphisms
            of the phase space (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+symplectomorphisms"} Hamiltonian symplectomorphisms
            mentioned above).
          %p
            As such, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"} quantomorphism group
            naturally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} acts
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+sections"} space of sections
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            is like a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunction"} wavefunction
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+coordinates"} canonical coordinates
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"} motivic quantization
            for more) it is indeed natural to choose a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"}> polarization
            ” of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+coordinates"} canonical coordinates
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+momenta"} canonical momenta
            and consider only those
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            which depend only on the former. These are the actual
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunctions"} wavefunctions
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"} quantomorphism group
            which preserves these polarized sections is the group of exponentiated
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            is the 2-dimensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+space"} symplectic vector space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+group"} Heisenberg group
            with its famous action by multiplication and differentiation operators on the space of complex-valued functions on the real line.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p#GeometricQuantization
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
          %p
            We had seen that every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
            induces a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+current"} presymplectic current
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi BFV
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_{BFV}
            (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EulerLagrangeOperatorForTivialVectorBundleOverMinkowskiSpacetime"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
            of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            in terms of which there is a concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+differential+forms"} Hamiltonian differential forms
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+fields"} Hamiltonian vector fields
            on the jet bundle (def.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HamiltonianForms"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            is induced by this
            = succeed "-structure." do
              %em
                local
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
          %p
            In order to disentangle the core concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            from the technicalities involved in fully fledged
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimensional
            situation.
          #GeometricRepresentationSchroedingerRepresentation.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+representation"} Schrödinger representation
                via
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            %p
              Consider the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CartesianSpacesAndSmoothFunctionsBetweenThem"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+functions"} coordinate functions
              denoted
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi q
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{q,p\}
              and to be called the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+coordinate"} canonical coordinate
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
              and its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+momentum"} canonical momentum
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              (as in example
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CanonicalMomentum"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+invariant+differential+form"} constant
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+2-form"} differential 2-form
              given in in
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:CanonicalMomentumPresymplecticCurrent"} (?)
              by
            #eq:R2SymplecticForm.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi d
                    %mi q
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\omega = d p \wedge d q
                    \\,.
            %p
              This is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+differential+form"} closed
              in that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi ω
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \omega = 0
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+vector+fields"} tangent vector fields
              into it (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ContractionOfFormsWithVectorFields"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+1-forms"} differential 1-forms
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            %p
              A choice of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+potential"} presymplectic potential
              for this
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
              is
            #eq:CanonicalSymplecticPotentialOnR2.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo −
                    %mi q
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi d
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\theta \;\coloneqq\; - q \, d p
            %p
              in that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi θ
                      %mo =
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \theta = \omega
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi θ
                      %mo =
                      %mi p
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi d
                      %mi q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta = p \, d q
            %p For
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\mathbb{R}^2
                    \\longrightarrow
                    \\mathbb{C}
            %p
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
              (an
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+field"} Hamiltonian vector field
              for it (as in def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HamiltonianForms"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+vector+field"} tangent vector field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_A
              (example
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TangentVectorFields"}
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ContractionOfFormsWithVectorFields"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:R2SymplecticForm"} (1)
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+differential"} de Rham differential
              of
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            #eq:HamiltonianVectorFieldOnR2.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mi A
                    %mi ω
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\iota_{v_A} \omega = d A
                    \\,.
            %p
              Consider the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foliation"} foliation
              of this phase space by constant-
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
              \-slices
            #eq:ConstantqSlicesOnR2.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (4)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Λ
                      %mi q
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Lambda_q \subset \mathbb{R}^2
                    \\,.
            %p
              These are also called the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/leaves"} leaves
              of a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+polarization"} real polarization
              of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            %p
              (Other choices of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarization
              are possible, notably the constant
              = succeed "-slices.)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %p We says that a smooth function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\psi \;\colon\; \mathbb{R}^2 \longrightarrow \mathbb{C}
            %p
              is
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarized
              if its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+derivative"} covariant derivative
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection on a bundle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mi θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \theta
              along the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/leaves"} leaves
              vanishes; which for the choice of polarization in
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ConstantqSlicesOnR2"} (4)
              means that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∇
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi p
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mphantom
                      %mi AAA
                    %mo ⇔
                    %mphantom
                      %mi AAA
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi p
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi d
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo +
                      %mi i
                      %mi θ
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\nabla_{\partial_p} \psi = 0
                    \\phantom{AAA}
                    \\Leftrightarrow
                    \\phantom{AAA}
                    \\iota_{\partial_p} \left(  d \psi + i \theta \psi \right)
                    \=
                    0
                    \\,,
            %p
              which in turn, for the choice of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+potential"} presymplectic potential
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:CanonicalSymplecticPotentialOnR2"} (2)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi p
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo −
                    %mi i
                    %mi q
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\frac{\partial}{\partial p} \psi - i q \psi = 0
                    \\,.
            %p
              The solutions to this
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
              are of the form
            #eq:PolarizedFunctionsForBasicExampleOnR2.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (5)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi q
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mi i
                    %mi p
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Psi(q,p) = \psi(q) \, \exp(+ i p q)
            %p
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi \colon \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{C}
              any
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+function"} wave function
            %p
              This establishes a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+isomorphism"} linear isomorphism
              between polarized smooth functions and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+functions"} wave functions
            %p
              By
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:HamiltonianVectorFieldOnR2"} (3)
              we have the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+fields"} Hamiltonian vector fields
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi q
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi p
                    %mphantom
                      %mi AAAA
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi p
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi q
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    v_q = \partial_p
                    \\phantom{AAAA}
                    v_p = -\partial_q
                    \\,.
            %p
              The corresponding
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
              is
            #eq:R2PoissonBracket.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (6)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mi q
                            %mo ,
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≔
                            %msub
                              %mi ι
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi v
                                  %mi p
                            %msub
                              %mi ι
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi v
                                  %mi q
                            %mi ω
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %msub
                              %mi ι
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mi q
                            %msub
                              %mi ι
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mi p
                            %mi d
                            %mi p
                            %mo ∧
                            %mi d
                            %mi q
                            %mo =
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    \\{q,p\}
                    \&amp; \coloneqq
                    \\iota_{v_p} \iota_{v_q}  \omega
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    \-\iota_{\partial_q} \iota_{\partial_p} d p \wedge d q
                    \=
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    \- 1
                    \\end{aligned}
            %p
              The action of the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+operator+%28in+geometric+quantization%29"} quantum operators
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat q
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat p
              on the polarized functions
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:PolarizedFunctionsForBasicExampleOnR2"} (5)
              is as follows
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mi q
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                            %mi Ψ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi q
                            %mo ,
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mi i
                            %msub
                              %mo ∇
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mi p
                            %mi Ψ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi q
                            %mo ,
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo +
                            %mi q
                            %mi Ψ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi q
                            %mo ,
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mi i
                            %munder
                              %munder
                                %mrow
                                  %mo (
                                  %munder
                                    %munder
                                      %mrow
                                        %mfrac
                                          %mo ∂
                                          %mrow
                                            %mo ∂
                                            %mi p
                                        %mrow
                                          %mo (
                                          %mi ψ
                                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                          %mi q
                                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                          %msup
                                            %mi e
                                            %mrow
                                              %mi i
                                              %mi q
                                              %mi p
                                          %mo )
                                      %mo ⏟
                                    %mrow
                                      %mo =
                                      %mi i
                                      %mi q
                                      %mi Ψ
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                      %mi q
                                      %mo ,
                                      %mi p
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                  %mo −
                                  %mi i
                                  %mi q
                                  %mi Ψ
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi q
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mi p
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                  %mo )
                                %mo ⏟
                              %mrow
                                %mo =
                                %mn 0
                            %mo +
                            %mi q
                            %mi Ψ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi q
                            %mo ,
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mrow
                              %mo (
                              %mi q
                              %mi ψ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi q
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo )
                            %msup
                              %mi e
                              %mrow
                                %mi i
                                %mi q
                                %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    \\hat q \Psi(q,p)
                    \&amp; =
                    \- i \nabla_{\partial_p}\Psi(q,p) + q \Psi(q,p)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = -i
                    \\underset{ = 0 }{
                    \\underbrace{
                    \\left(
                    \\underset{
                    \= i q \Psi(q,p)
                    }{
                    \\underbrace{
                    \\frac{\partial}{\partial p}
                    \\left(
                    \\psi(q) e^{i q p}
                    \\right)
                    }
                    }
                    \- i q \Psi(q,p)
                    \\right)
                    }
                    }
                    +
                    q \Psi(q,p)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = \left( q \psi(q) \right) e^{i q p}
                    \\end{aligned}
            %p and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mi p
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                            %mi Ψ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi q
                            %mo ,
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mi i
                            %msub
                              %mo ∇
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mi q
                            %mi Ψ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi q
                            %mo ,
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo +
                            %mi p
                            %mi Ψ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi q
                            %mo ,
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mi i
                            %mfrac
                              %mo ∂
                              %mrow
                                %mo ∂
                                %mi q
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ψ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi q
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %msup
                              %mi e
                              %mrow
                                %mi i
                                %mi q
                                %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo +
                            %mi p
                            %mi Ψ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi q
                            %mo ,
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mrow
                              %mo (
                              %mi i
                              %mfrac
                                %mo ∂
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mi q
                              %mi ψ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi q
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo )
                            %msup
                              %mi e
                              %mrow
                                %mi i
                                %mi q
                                %mi p
                            %mo +
                            %munder
                              %munder
                                %mrow
                                  %munder
                                    %munder
                                      %mrow
                                        %mi ψ
                                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                        %mi q
                                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                        %mrow
                                          %mo (
                                          %mi i
                                          %mfrac
                                            %mo ∂
                                            %mrow
                                              %mo ∂
                                              %mi q
                                          %msup
                                            %mi e
                                            %mrow
                                              %mi i
                                              %mi q
                                              %mi p
                                          %mo )
                                      %mo ⏟
                                    %mrow
                                      %mo =
                                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                      %mi p
                                      %mi Ψ
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                      %mi q
                                      %mo ,
                                      %mi p
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                  %mo +
                                  %mi p
                                  %mi Ψ
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi q
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mi p
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo ⏟
                              %mrow
                                %mo =
                                %mn 0
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mrow
                              %mo (
                              %mi i
                              %mfrac
                                %mo ∂
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mi q
                              %mi ψ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi q
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo )
                            %msup
                              %mi e
                              %mrow
                                %mi i
                                %mi p
                                %mi q
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    \\hat p \Psi(q,p)
                    \&amp; =
                    i \nabla_{\partial_q} \Psi(q,p) + p \Psi(q,p)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = i \frac{\partial}{\partial q} (\psi(q)e^{i q p}) + p \Psi(q,p)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    \\left(
                    i \frac{\partial}{\partial q}\psi(q)
                    \\right) e^{i q p}
                    +
                    \\underset{ = 0}{
                    \\underbrace{
                    \\underset{
                    \= - p \Psi(q,p)
                    }{
                    \\underbrace{
                    \\psi(q)
                    \\left(
                    i \frac{\partial}{\partial q} e^{i q p}
                    \\right)
                    }
                    }
                    +
                    p \Psi(q,p)
                    }
                    }
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    \\left(
                    i \frac{\partial}{\partial q}\psi(q)
                    \\right) e^{i p q}
                    \\end{aligned}
                    \\,.
            %p
              Hence under the identification
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:PolarizedFunctionsForBasicExampleOnR2"} (5)
              we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo =
                    %mi q
                    %mi ψ
                    %mphantom
                      %mi AAAA
                    %mover
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo =
                    %mi i
                    %mfrac
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi q
                    %mi ψ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\hat q \psi = q \psi
                    \\phantom{AAAA}
                    \\hat p \psi = i \frac{\partial}{\partial q} \psi
                    \\,.
            %p
              This is called the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+representation"} Schrödinger representation
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+commutation+relation"} canonical commutation relation
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:R2PoissonBracket"} (6)
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p#StarProducts
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moyal+star+products"} Moyal star products
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+space"} finite dimensional vector space
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \in V \otimes V
            be an element of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            (not necessarily skew symmetric at the moment).
          %p
            We may canonically regard
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            is canonically regarded as a constant rank-2
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor"} tensor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            by forming
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivatives"} derivatives
            on the tensor product of the space of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi
                  \\;\colon\;
                  C^\infty(V) \otimes C^\infty(V)
                  \\longrightarrow
                  C^\infty(V) \otimes C^\infty(V)
                  \\,.
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\partial_i\}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+basis"} linear basis
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma(T V)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi j
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi(f \otimes g)
                  \\;=\;
                  \\pi^{i j}
                  (\partial_i f) \otimes (\partial_j g)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mi f
                  %mo ≔
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_i f \coloneqq \frac{\partial f}{\partial x^i}
            denotes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+derivative"} partial derivative
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            along the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate"} coordinate
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+summation+convention"} Einstein summation convention
          %p For emphasis we write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mn ∞
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⊗
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mn ∞
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mi prod
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mn ∞
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi f
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi g
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↦
                        %mtd
                          %mi f
                          %mo ⋅
                          %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  C^\infty(V) \otimes C^\infty(V)
                  \&amp;\overset{prod}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                  C^\infty(V)
                  \\\
                  f \otimes g &amp;\mapsto&amp; f \cdot g
                  }
          %p for the pointwise product of smooth functions.
          #StarPoduct.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
                induced by constant rank-2
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor"} tensor
            %p
              Given
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,\pi)
              as above, then the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
              induced by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series+algebra"} formal power series algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(V) [ [\hbar] ]
              in a formal variable
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
              (“
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"}> Planck's constant
              ”) with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              is the linear map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (-) \star_\pi (-)
                    \\;\colon\;
                    C^\infty(V)[ [ \hbar ] ] \otimes C^\infty(V)[ [ \hbar ] ]
                    \\longrightarrow
                    C^\infty(V)[ [\hbar] ]
            %p given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi prod
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi exp
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi ℏ
                      %msup
                        %mi π
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mi j
                      %mfrac
                        %mo ∂
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∂
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mi i
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mfrac
                        %mo ∂
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∂
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mi j
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (-) \star_\pi (-)
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    prod
                    \\circ \exp\left(
                    \\hbar \pi^{i j} \frac{\partial}{\partial x^i} \otimes \frac{\partial}{\partial x^j}
                    \\right)
            %p Hence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mi π
                    %mi g
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi ℏ
                    %msup
                      %mi π
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi j
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mi i
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mi j
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi ℏ
                      %mn 2
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mi π
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi j
                    %msup
                      %mi π
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mi l
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo ∂
                          %mn 2
                        %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mi i
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mi k
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo ∂
                          %mn 2
                        %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mi j
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mi l
                    %mo +
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \star_\pi g
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    1
                    +
                    \\hbar \pi^{i j}
                    \\frac{\partial f}{\partial x^i} \cdot \frac{\partial g}{\partial x^j}
                    +
                    \\hbar^2
                    \\tfrac{ 1 }{2}
                    \\pi^{i j}
                    \\pi^{k l}
                    \\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^{i} \partial x^{k}}
                    \\cdot
                    \\frac{\partial^2 g}{\partial x^{j} \partial x^{l}}
                    +
                    \\cdots
                    \\,.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
                degenerating to pointwise product)
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi = 0
              in def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StarPoduct"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mo ⋅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star_0 = \cdot
              is the plain pointwise product of functions.
          #MoyalStarProduct.num_exaple
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moyal+star+product"}> Moyal star product
                )
            %p
              If the tensor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StarPoduct"}
              is skew-symmetric, it may be regarded as a constant
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+tensor"} Poisson tensor
              on the smooth manifold
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                          %mfrac
                            %mn 1
                            %mn 2
                        %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star_{\tfrac{1}{2}\pi}
              is called a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moyal+star+product"} Moyal star product
              and the star-product algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(V)[ [\hbar] ], \star_\pi)
              is called the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moyal+deformation+quantization"} Moyal deformation quantization
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo ,
                      %mi π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,\pi)
          #AssociativeAndUnitalStarProduct.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associative
                and
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitality"} unital
            %p
              Given
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,\pi)
              as above, then the star product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-) \star_\pi (-)
              from def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StarPoduct"}
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associative
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitality"} unital
              with unit the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+function"} constant function
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↪
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi ℏ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \in C^\infty(V) \hookrightarrow C^\infty(V)[ [ \hbar ] ]
            %p
              Hence the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(V)
              equipped with the star product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unital+algebra"} unital
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Observe that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+rule"} product rule
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiation"} differentiation
              says that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi prod
                    %mo =
                    %mi prod
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi i
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi id
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo +
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi id
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\partial_i \circ prod
                    \=
                    prod \circ ( \partial_i \otimes id \;+\; id \otimes \partial_i  )
                    \\,.
            %p Using this we compute as follows:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %msub
                              %mo ⋆
                              %mi π
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %msub
                              %mo ⋆
                              %mi π
                            %mi h
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mi prod
                            %mo ∘
                            %mi exp
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi π
                              %mrow
                                %mi i
                                %mi j
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi i
                            %mo ⊗
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∘
                            %mrow
                              %mo (
                              %mrow
                                %mo (
                                %mi prod
                                %mo ∘
                                %mi exp
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %msup
                                  %mi π
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi k
                                    %mi l
                                %msub
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mi k
                                %mo ⊗
                                %msub
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mi l
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo )
                              %mo ⊗
                              %mi id
                              %mo )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi g
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mi prod
                            %mo ∘
                            %mi exp
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi π
                              %mrow
                                %mi i
                                %mi j
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi i
                            %mo ⊗
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∘
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi prod
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi id
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∘
                            %mrow
                              %mo (
                              %mi exp
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msup
                                %mi π
                                %mrow
                                  %mi k
                                  %mi l
                              %msub
                                %mo ∂
                                %mi k
                              %mo ⊗
                              %msub
                                %mo ∂
                                %mi l
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo ⊗
                              %mi id
                              %mo )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi g
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mi prod
                            %mo ∘
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi prod
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi id
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∘
                            %mi exp
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi π
                              %mrow
                                %mi i
                                %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi i
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi id
                            %mo ⊗
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi j
                            %mo +
                            %mi id
                            %mo ⊗
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi i
                            %mo ⊗
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∘
                            %mi exp
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi π
                              %mrow
                                %mi k
                                %mi l
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi k
                            %mo ⊗
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi l
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi id
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi g
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mi prod
                            %mo ∘
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi prod
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi id
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∘
                            %mi exp
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi π
                              %mrow
                                %mi i
                                %mi j
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi i
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi id
                            %mo ⊗
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∘
                            %mi exp
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi π
                              %mrow
                                %mi i
                                %mi j
                            %mi id
                            %mo ⊗
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi i
                            %mo ⊗
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∘
                            %mi exp
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi π
                              %mrow
                                %mi k
                                %mi l
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi k
                            %mo ⊗
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi l
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi id
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi g
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %msub
                              %mi prod
                              %mn 3
                            %mo ∘
                            %mi exp
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi π
                              %mrow
                                %mi i
                                %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi i
                            %mo ⊗
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi j
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi id
                            %mo +
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi i
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi id
                            %mo ⊗
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi j
                            %mo +
                            %mi id
                            %mo ⊗
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi i
                            %mo ⊗
                            %msub
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    \&amp; (f \star_\pi  g) \star_\pi h
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    prod \circ
                    \\exp( \pi^{i j} \partial_i \otimes \partial_j )  \circ
                    \\left(
                    \\left( prod \circ \exp( \pi^{k l} \partial_k \otimes \partial_l ) \right) \otimes id
                    \\right)
                    (f \otimes g \otimes g)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    prod \circ
                    \\exp( \pi^{i j} \partial_i \otimes \partial_j ) \circ
                    (prod \otimes id)
                    \\circ
                    \\left(
                    \\exp( \pi^{k l} \partial_k \otimes \partial_l )  \otimes id
                    \\right)
                    (f \otimes g \otimes g)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    prod \circ
                    (prod \otimes id) \circ
                    \\exp( \pi^{i j}  ( \partial_i \otimes id \otimes \partial_j +id \otimes \partial_i \otimes \partial_j   )
                    \\circ
                    \\exp( \pi^{k l} \partial_k \otimes \partial_l )  \otimes id
                    (f \otimes g \otimes g)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    prod \circ
                    (prod \otimes id) \circ
                    \\exp( \pi^{i j}   \partial_i \otimes id \otimes \partial_j   )
                    \\circ
                    \\exp( \pi^{i j}  id \otimes \partial_i \otimes \partial_j )
                    \\circ
                    \\exp( \pi^{k l} \partial_k \otimes \partial_l \otimes id )
                    (f \otimes g \otimes g)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    prod_3
                    \\circ
                    \\exp( \pi^{i j} ( \partial_i \otimes \partial_j \otimes id  +  \partial_i \otimes id \otimes \partial_j + id \otimes \partial_i \otimes \partial_j)   )
                    \\end{aligned}
            %p
              In the last line we used that the ordinary pointwise product of functions is associative, and wrote
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi prod
                      %mn 3
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} prod_3 \colon C^\infty(V) \otimes C^\infty(V) \otimes C^\infty(V) \to C^\infty(V)
              for the unique pointwise product of three functions.
            %p The last expression above is manifestly independent of the choice of order of the arguments in the triple star product, and hence it is clear that an analogous computation yields
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mi π
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\cdots = f \star_\pi (g \star_\pi h)
                    \\,.
          #SymmetricContribution.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (shift by symmetric contribution is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
                of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+products"} star products
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              be a vector space,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \in V \otimes V
              a rank-2
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor"} tensor
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Sym
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in Sym(V \otimes V)
              a
              %em symmetric
              rank-2 tensor.
            %p
              Then the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi C
                              %mn ∞
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi V
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %mi exp
                                %mrow
                                  %mo (
                                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                                    %mfrac
                                      %mn 1
                                      %mn 2
                                  %mi α
                                  %mo )
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi C
                              %mn ∞
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi V
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↦
                          %mtd
                            %mi exp
                            %mrow
                              %mo (
                              %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                                %mfrac
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mn 2
                              %mi ℏ
                              %msup
                                %mi α
                                %mrow
                                  %mi i
                                  %mi j
                              %msub
                                %mo ∂
                                %mi i
                              %msub
                                %mo ∂
                                %mi j
                              %mo )
                            %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    C^\infty(V)
                    \&amp;\overset{\exp\left(\tfrac{1}{2}\alpha \right)}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                    C^\infty(V)
                    \\\
                    f
                    \&amp;\mapsto&amp;
                    \\exp\left( \tfrac{1}{2}\hbar \alpha^{i j} \partial_i \partial_j \right) f
                    }
            %p
              constitutes an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              of star product algebras (prop.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AssociativeAndUnitalStarProduct"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi exp
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi ℏ
                      %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mfrac
                          %mn 1
                          %mn 2
                      %mi ℏ
                      %mi α
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mrow
                        %mi π
                        %mo +
                        %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\exp\left(\hbar\tfrac{1}{2}\hbar\alpha \right)
                    \\;\colon\;
                    (C^\infty(V)[ [\hbar] ], \star_{\pi})
                    \\overset{\simeq}{\longrightarrow}
                    (C^\infty(V))[ [\hbar] ], \star_{\pi + \alpha})
                    \\,,
            %p
              hence identifying the star product induced from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              with that induced from
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                      %mo +
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi + \alpha
            %p
              In particular every star product algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi C
